#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
dunenv - DUNE SL7 container + software environment launcher.

One command for the whole workflow that duneSL7_setup.sh does:

    host shell                         inside the SL7 container
    ----------                         ------------------------
    dunenv                (menu)       dunenv                (menu)
    dunenv start [ENV] [build]  --->   (setup runs automatically)
    dunenv shell [build]               dunenv setup [ENV]
    dunenv list                        dunenv justin
    dunenv add NAME AREA               dunenv status
    dunenv new NAME DIR -v VER  (create + build an MRB area, dunebuild* only)
    dunenv rebuild NAME [--clean]  (recompile a registered area, dunebuild* only)

Everything that can change lives in dunenv.ini (next to this file):
dunesw version/qualifier, extra UPS packages, exported variables, container
image and binds, and the local MRB environments ([env:<name>] sections).

How the pieces fit together
---------------------------
1. This Python file never touches the shell environment itself. It reads
   dunenv.ini, decides what to set up (the "plan"), and writes a bash script
   that does it.
2. "start" launches apptainer with  bash --rcfile dunenv.sh -i  and passes the
   bash script through the DUNENV_AUTORUN variable (base64 encoded, so no
   character in it can be mangled on the way into the container). dunenv.sh
   decodes and runs it inside the new container shell.
3. Inside a shell, "setup" / "justin" must change *that* shell, so they go
   through the "dunenv" bash function defined in dunenv.sh: the function asks
   this program to write the script to a temporary file (DUNENV_EMIT), sources
   it, and deletes it.

Written for the Python 3.6 in the SL7 container and the 3.9 on the EL9 host.
"""

